text:
To turn this mock-up into a colorful website, you can create an HTML file with a container that includes a list of jokes. You can then use CSS to style the container, add colors, and create a layout. The jokes themselves can be displayed using HTML elements like `<p>` and `<ul>`.

Here's a simple example of the HTML structure:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>My Joke Website</title>
  <style>
    body {
      background-color: #f2f2f2;
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    }

    .joke-container {
      margin: 20px 0;
      padding: 20px;
      border: 1px solid #000;
      border-radius: 5px;
      background-color: #fff;
      box-shadow: 0px 0px 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
    }

    .joke-title {
      font-weight: bold;
      font-size: 24px;
      margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    .joke {
      font-size: 18px;
      line-height: 1.5;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="joke-container">
    <h2>Jokes:</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>Isaac Newton once said, 'I can calculate the motion of heavenly bodies, but not the madness of people.'</p>
        <p>Batman once said, 'I'm not a hero, I'm a detective.'</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
```
